# Infrastructure

The Terraform configuration in the root of the repository creates the
resources below in `${{ values.awsRegion or 'us-east-1' }}`. Names start with
`${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}`.

| Path | Contents |
| ---- | -------- |
| `main.tf` | Names and tags{% if vpc %}, network{% endif %}{% if values.enableDatabase %}, database{% endif %}{% if values.enableCache %}, cache{% endif %}{% if values.enableStorage %}, bucket{% endif %} |
| `function.tf` | Function, its execution role, the alias `live`{% if gradual %}, CodeDeploy{% endif %} |
| `api.tf` | ${{ apiName }}, authentication{% if waf %}, web ACL{% endif %}{% if cloudfront %}, CloudFront distribution{% endif %} |
| `monitoring.tf` | Log group of the function{% if values.enableMetrics %}, metric filters{% endif %}{% if values.enableAlarms %}, alarms and their topic{% endif %} |
| `variables.tf` | Inputs; the values chosen in the portal are the defaults |
| `outputs.tf` | Outputs |
| `tests/` | Plan of the configuration against a mocked AWS provider, run by `terraform test` |
{%- if interpreted %}
| `placeholder/` | Handler that is deployed until a function package is supplied |
{%- endif %}
{%- if keyAuthorizer or oidcAuthorizer %}
| `authorizer/` | Source of the Lambda authorizer of the API |
{%- endif %}
{%- if apiType == 'graphql' %}
| `graphql/schema.graphql` | Schema of the API |
{%- endif %}
{%- if gradual %}
| `scripts/shift-traffic.sh` | Moves traffic to a new function version with CodeDeploy |
{%- endif %}

Shared modules are taken from
`github.com/ayoinc/cloud-sandbox-backstage` at a pinned commit.

## Function

- Lambda function `${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-api`, runtime
  `${{ {'nodejs24': 'nodejs24.x', 'python313': 'python3.13', 'go126': 'provided.al2023', 'dotnet10': 'dotnet10', 'java21': 'java21'}[runtime] }}`, ${{ values.memorySize or 256 }} MB, timeout ${{ values.timeout or 30 }} s.
- An execution role that may write to the log group of the function
  {%- if values.enableTracing %}, send traces to X-Ray{% endif %}
  {%- if values.enableMetrics %}, publish metrics in the namespace `${{ values.projectName }}/${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}`{% endif %}
  {%- if nosql %}, read and write the DynamoDB table{% endif %}
  {%- if sql %}, read the database secret{% endif %}
  {%- if values.enableStorage %}, read and write objects in the bucket{% endif %}
  {%- if vpc %}, manage its network interfaces in the VPC{% endif %} and nothing else.
- Log group `/aws/lambda/${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-api`, kept for ${{ values.logRetentionDays or 30 }} days.
- Every apply that changes the code or the configuration publishes a new
  function version. The API calls the alias `live`.
{%- if values.deploymentStrategy == 'canary' %}
- Deployment strategy `canary`: a CodeDeploy application and deployment group
  move the alias to a new version with
  `CodeDeployDefault.LambdaCanary10Percent5Minutes` (10 % of the traffic for five
  minutes, then the rest). See [Traffic shifting](#traffic-shifting).
{%- elif values.deploymentStrategy == 'linear' %}
- Deployment strategy `linear`: a CodeDeploy application and deployment group
  move the alias to a new version with
  `CodeDeployDefault.LambdaLinear10PercentEvery1Minute` (10 % more of the traffic
  every minute). See [Traffic shifting](#traffic-shifting).
{%- else %}
- Deployment strategy `all-at-once`: the alias moves to the new version in the
  same apply.
{%- endif %}

The function receives these environment variables:

| Variable | Value |
| -------- | ----- |
| `PROJECT_NAME`, `ENVIRONMENT` | Project name and environment |
{%- if values.enableCors and apiType == 'rest' %}
| `CORS_ALLOW_ORIGIN` | Origin to return in `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableMetrics %}
| `METRICS_NAMESPACE` | CloudWatch namespace the function may publish metrics in |
{%- endif %}
{%- if nosql %}
| `TABLE_NAME` | DynamoDB table |
{%- endif %}
{%- if sql %}
| `DATABASE_HOST`, `DATABASE_PORT`, `DATABASE_NAME` | Writer endpoint of the Aurora cluster |
| `DATABASE_SECRET_ARN` | Secrets Manager secret with the user name and password |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableCache %}
| `CACHE_HOST`, `CACHE_PORT`, `CACHE_TLS` | Primary endpoint of the cache; connections use TLS |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableStorage %}
| `BUCKET_NAME` | S3 bucket |
{%- endif %}

Add your own with the variable `function_environment`.

## Function package

The repository holds no application code. The code is built elsewhere and
handed to Terraform as a zip file in one of two ways:

- `deployment_package`: path to a zip file on the machine that runs Terraform.
- `deployment_package_s3`: `{ bucket = "...", key = "..." }` of a zip file in
  S3, optionally with `object_version`. Use this when Terraform runs in a
  pipeline: upload the package from the application's pipeline and commit the
  new key or object version to `terraform.tfvars` here. Terraform only notices
  a new package when the key or the object version changes.

Set `function_handler` to the entry point of your package (default
`${{ {'nodejs24': 'index.handler', 'python313': 'index.handler', 'go126': 'bootstrap', 'dotnet10': 'Api::Api.Function::FunctionHandler', 'java21': 'example.Handler::handleRequest'}[runtime] }}`) and `function_architecture` to `arm64` if it is built for
Graviton.
{%- if interpreted %}

While neither variable is set, Terraform packages the placeholder handler in
`placeholder/` and deploys it, so the first apply works without a build. The
placeholder answers
{%- if apiType == 'graphql' %} the query `status` of `graphql/schema.graphql`
{%- elif apiType == 'rest' %} every request with status 200 and a JSON body, in the response format of
a REST API proxy integration
{%- else %} every request with status 200 and a JSON body, in the response format of
HTTP API payload version 2.0
{%- endif %}.
{%- else %}

The `${{ runtime }}` runtime needs a built artefact, so there is no placeholder:
`terraform plan` stops with an error that names these variables until one of
them is set. `terraform validate` and the CI workflow do not need the package.
{%- if runtime == 'go126' %}
The zip file must contain the executable under the name `bootstrap`.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if apiType == 'graphql' %}

The function is called once per resolved field with the AppSync context object
(`info.parentTypeName`, `info.fieldName`, `arguments`, `identity`) and returns
the value of the field.
{%- elif apiType == 'rest' %}

The function is called with REST API proxy events (payload format 1.0) and
returns `statusCode`, `headers` and `body`. API Gateway waits at most 29
seconds for it.
{%- else %}

The function is called with HTTP API events of payload format 2.0. API Gateway
waits at most 30 seconds for it.
{%- endif %}

## API

{%- if apiType == 'http' %}

- API Gateway HTTP API with one `$default` route that sends every request to
  the function, and a `$default` stage that deploys changes automatically.
- Access logs in `/aws/apigateway/${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-api`, kept for ${{ values.logRetentionDays or 30 }} days.
{%- elif apiType == 'rest' %}

- Regional API Gateway REST API. The root path and `/{proxy+}` send every
  method to the function. The stage is named `${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}`.
- Access logs are off by default because a REST API can only write them once
  the account has an API Gateway CloudWatch Logs role, which is a setting shared
  by all APIs of the account and region. After configuring that role set
  `api_access_logging = true`.
{%- else %}

- AppSync GraphQL API with the schema in `graphql/schema.graphql`, a Lambda data
  source for the function and a direct Lambda resolver for `Query.status`. Add
  an `aws_appsync_resolver` to `api.tf` for every further field the function
  resolves.
- Field errors are logged to `/aws/appsync/apis/<api id>`, kept for ${{ values.logRetentionDays or 30 }} days.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableCors %}
{%- if apiType == 'http' %}
- CORS: API Gateway answers preflight requests and adds the CORS headers for
  the origins in `cors_allowed_origins` (default: any).
{%- elif apiType == 'rest' %}
- CORS: API Gateway answers `OPTIONS` requests itself and adds
  `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to the errors it produces. Responses of the
  function must carry the header themselves; the function finds the value of
  `cors_allowed_origin` (default `*`) in `CORS_ALLOW_ORIGIN`.
{%- else %}
- CORS: AppSync answers preflight requests and allows every origin. This is
  fixed behaviour of AppSync, so there is nothing to configure.
{%- endif %}
{%- elif apiType == 'graphql' %}
- CORS was not requested, but AppSync always answers preflight requests and
  allows every origin. This cannot be turned off; browsers of any origin can
  call the API if they hold valid credentials.
{%- else %}
- CORS is not configured: browsers refuse calls from pages of other origins.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableThrottling %}
{%- if apiType == 'graphql' %}
- Throttling: AppSync has no rate or burst setting. Instead a rate-based rule
  in the web ACL blocks a client IP address that sends more than
  `throttle_rate_limit` × 300 requests in five minutes (default
  ${{ values.throttleRateLimit or 1000 }} requests per second). The limit is per address, not for the API as a
  whole, and the burst limit of the form is not used.
{%- else %}
- Throttling: ${{ values.throttleRateLimit or 1000 }} requests per second with a burst of ${{ values.throttleBurstLimit or 2000 }} for the whole
  stage (`throttle_rate_limit`, `throttle_burst_limit`).
  {%- if apiType == 'rest' and authType == 'api-key' %} The usage plan applies
  the same limits to each API key.{% endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- else %}
- Throttling is not configured: only the account quotas of the service apply.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableTracing %}
{%- if apiType == 'http' %}
- Tracing: the function sends traces to X-Ray. HTTP APIs cannot take part in
  X-Ray traces, so a trace starts at the function.
{%- else %}
- Tracing: the API and the function send traces to X-Ray.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

### Authentication
{%- if authType == 'jwt' %}

A Cognito user pool (`${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-users`, sign-in with e-mail address, optional
TOTP as second factor) and a public app client are created. Users are created
by an administrator unless `allow_self_sign_up` is `true`.
{%- if apiType == 'http' %}
A JWT authorizer accepts ID and access tokens of the pool in the
`Authorization` header.
{%- elif apiType == 'rest' %}
A Cognito authorizer accepts the ID token of a user in the `Authorization`
header.
{%- else %}
The API uses the pool as its authorization provider; send the token in the
`Authorization` header.
{%- endif %}
{%- elif authType == 'oauth2' %}

Tokens of an external OpenID Connect provider are accepted. Two variables have
no default and must be set before the first plan, for example in
`terraform.tfvars`:

```hcl
oauth2_issuer   = "https://login.example.com/"
oauth2_audience = "api://${{ values.projectName }}"
```

`oauth2_issuer` must equal the `iss` claim of the tokens and serve
`/.well-known/openid-configuration`.
{%- if apiType == 'http' %}
A JWT authorizer checks signature, issuer, audience and expiry of the bearer
token in the `Authorization` header.
{%- elif apiType == 'rest' %}

REST APIs have no authorizer for external OpenID Connect providers. A Lambda
authorizer (`authorizer/index.mjs`, function
`${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-authorizer`) is used instead: it fetches the signing keys of the
issuer and checks signature (RS256, RS384, RS512, ES256, ES384), issuer,
audience and expiry of the bearer token. API Gateway caches its decision for
five minutes per token.
{%- else %}
AppSync verifies the token in the `Authorization` header (sent without a
`Bearer` prefix) against the issuer and requires the audience in the `aud`
claim.
{%- endif %}
{%- elif authType == 'api-key' %}
{%- if apiType == 'http' %}

HTTP APIs have no API keys or usage plans. A Lambda authorizer
(`authorizer/index.mjs`, function `${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-authorizer`) is used
instead: Terraform generates one key, the authorizer compares the `x-api-key`
header with it, and API Gateway caches the decision for five minutes. Read the
key with `terraform output -raw api_key`; replace it with
`terraform apply -replace=random_password.api_key`.
{%- elif apiType == 'rest' %}

An API key and a usage plan are created and every method requires a key in the
`x-api-key` header. Read the key with `terraform output -raw api_key`.
{%- else %}

The API uses API key authorization. Read the key with
`terraform output -raw api_key` and send it in the `x-api-key` header.
{%- endif %}
{%- elif authType == 'iam' %}

Requests must be signed with AWS Signature Version 4 by an IAM principal that
is allowed
{%- if apiType == 'graphql' %} `appsync:GraphQL` on the API.
{%- else %} `execute-api:Invoke` on the API.
{%- endif %}
{%- if cloudfront %}
Because the API is called through CloudFront (see below), sign the request for
the host of `api_origin_endpoint` and send it to `api_endpoint`: CloudFront
replaces the `Host` header with the host of the API before forwarding.
{%- endif %}
{%- else %}
{%- if apiType == 'graphql' %}

AppSync has no unauthenticated mode. For the choice "none" the API uses API
key authorization and the key is meant to be shipped with the clients: read it
with `terraform output -raw api_key` and send it in the `x-api-key` header.
{%- else %}

None: the API is public.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if graphqlKey %}

An AppSync key is valid for at most 365 days. The output `api_key_expires`
shows the end date; applying the configuration more than 300 days after the
last renewal moves it a year ahead without changing the key.
{%- endif %}
{%- if waf %}

### Web application firewall
{%- if values.enableWaf %}

A web ACL with the AWS managed rule groups Common, Known Bad Inputs, SQL
injection, Linux and Amazon IP reputation list filters the requests. The Common
rule group blocks request bodies larger than 8 KB among other things; adjust
`managed_rule_groups` of the module `waf` in `api.tf` if the API needs
different rules.
{%- if apiType == 'http' %}

A web ACL cannot be attached to an HTTP API. A CloudFront distribution is
placed in front of the API instead and carries the web ACL (created in
us-east-1, as CloudFront requires). It forwards every request without caching.
Clients must call the output `api_endpoint` (the `cloudfront.net` address). The
address of the API itself (`api_origin_endpoint`) stays reachable and is not
protected by the web ACL, so treat it as internal. The `cloudfront.net`
certificate accepts TLS 1.0 and later; a stricter policy needs a custom domain
with its own certificate, which this configuration does not set up.
{%- else %}
The web ACL is attached to the ${{ 'stage of the API' if apiType == 'rest' else 'API' }}.
{%- endif %}
{%- else %}

WAF was not requested. The web ACL exists only for the rate limit described
under throttling and contains no other rule.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableDatabase or values.enableCache or values.enableStorage %}

## Data
{% if nosql %}
- DynamoDB table `${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-data` with partition key `pk` and sort key `sk`
  (strings), on-demand capacity, encryption at rest and point-in-time recovery.
  Deletion protection is on in production.
{%- endif %}
{%- if sql %}
- Aurora Serverless v2 cluster (PostgreSQL, the engine version AWS currently
  offers as default) with one instance, 0.5 to 2 capacity units
  (`database_min_capacity`, `database_max_capacity`), storage encrypted with
  its own KMS key (`alias/${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-database`) and a database named `app`. RDS keeps the password of the user `dbadmin` in Secrets
  Manager and rotates it; the function may read that secret. Create a
  less-privileged database user for the application once the schema exists.
  Production keeps 14 days of backups, has deletion protection and takes a
  final snapshot; other environments keep 7 days and are deleted without one.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableCache %}
- ElastiCache replication group for Redis OSS 7.1 (`cache_node_type`, default
  `cache.t3.micro`) with encryption at rest and in transit. Production has a
  primary and a replica in two availability zones with automatic failover;
  other environments have a single node. The replication group is named
  `${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-redis`; the project part is shortened if the name would exceed
  the 40 characters ElastiCache allows.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableStorage %}
- S3 bucket `${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-<account id>` with versioning, encryption at rest, all
  public access blocked and a policy that refuses requests without TLS.
{%- endif %}
{%- if vpc %}

### Network

{% if sql and values.enableCache %}The database and the cache are{% elif sql %}The database is{% else %}The cache is{% endif %} only reachable inside a VPC, so the
function is attached to it:

- VPC `vpc_cidr` (default `10.0.0.0/16`) with public, private and database
  subnets in three availability zones, flow logs, and gateway endpoints for S3
  and DynamoDB.
- The function runs in the private subnets. It reaches AWS APIs and the
  internet through a NAT gateway: one per availability zone in production, a
  single one in other environments.
- Security groups allow the function to open connections to
  {%- if sql %} the database on port 5432{% endif %}
  {%- if sql and values.enableCache %} and{% endif %}
  {%- if values.enableCache %} the cache on port 6379{% endif %} and to any address on port 443.
  Nothing else may connect to {% if sql and values.enableCache %}the database or the cache{% elif sql %}the database{% else %}the cache{% endif %}.

A function in a VPC starts more slowly the first time, and the NAT gateway and
the {% if sql and values.enableCache %}database and cache{% elif sql %}database{% else %}cache{% endif %} are billed while idle.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

## Observability

- Logs of the function are kept for ${{ values.logRetentionDays or 30 }} days (`log_retention_days`).
{%- if values.enableTracing %}
- X-Ray tracing is active on the function{% if apiType != 'http' %} and the API{% endif %}.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableMetrics %}
- Custom metrics use the namespace `${{ values.projectName }}/${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}`. Metric filters count
  log lines of the function that contain `ERROR`, `Error` or `Exception`
  (`LoggedErrors`) and invocations that ran into the timeout (`Timeouts`). The
  function may publish its own metrics in the same namespace, either with
  `PutMetricData` or in embedded metric format.
  {%- if apiType == 'graphql' %} AppSync publishes enhanced metrics per
  resolver, data source and operation.
  {%- else %} API Gateway publishes detailed metrics per route.
  {%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableAlarms %}
- Alarms notify the SNS topic `${{ values.projectName }}-${{ values.environment or 'dev' }}-alarms`: function errors, function
  throttles, 5xx responses of the API (each: any in five minutes) and average
  API latency above `alarm_latency_threshold_ms` (default 2000) for fifteen
  minutes. Set `alarm_email` to subscribe an address; the recipient must
  confirm the subscription.
{%- endif %}

